+# Product-promise comprehension research template
+
+Use this lightweight, moderated template to measure whether people understand ForkMesh's product promise before a release or major positioning change.
+Run it with the current public product, the proof path, and a consistent moderator script.
+
+## Promise shown to participants
+
+> ForkMesh lets developers collaborate on, mirror, and preserve source-code repositories across independent hosts—without relying on any single hosting provider.
+
+Show the sentence without explaining its terms first.
+Ask the questions below before demonstrating the product or proof path.
+
+## Recommended cohorts
+
+Run a separate five-person session for each of these cohorts:
+
+- 5 maintainers
+- 5 contributors
+- 5 operators/node runners
+
+Record the cohort type and a randomized participant ID only.
+Do not record names, email addresses, repository names, or other identifying information.
+
+## Moderator script
+
+Tell the participant that you are testing the clarity of the product sentence, not their technical knowledge.
+Read each question exactly as written and do not explain, correct, or prompt for missing terms until all answers are recorded.
+After the questions, invite the participant to use the public proof path if they want to explore the product.
+
+## Questions
+
+1. In your words, what does ForkMesh do?
+2. What are the three jobs this sentence promises?
+3. What does ‘without relying on any single hosting provider’ mean?
+4. What would you try first to prove it works?
+
+## Pass rule
+
+A participant passes only when their unprompted response mentions all three jobs: collaborate, mirror, and preserve.
+A participant also must understand the benefit as no single provider controls availability.
+A cohort passes when at least 4 of 5 participants pass, which is 80%.
+Score every intended cohort separately rather than combining answers across cohorts.
+
+## Anonymized session record
+
+Copy this table once per cohort and replace the placeholder IDs with randomized IDs such as `M-01`, `C-01`, or `O-01`.
+
+| Participant ID | Collaborate | Mirror | Preserve | No single provider controls availability | First proof attempt | Pass | Notes without identifiers |
+| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
+| `XX-01` |  |  |  |  |  |  |  |
+| `XX-02` |  |  |  |  |  |  |  |
+| `XX-03` |  |  |  |  |  |  |  |
+| `XX-04` |  |  |  |  |  |  |  |
+| `XX-05` |  |  |  |  |  |  |  |
+
+Mark each understanding column as `yes`, `no`, or `unclear`.
+Mark Pass as `yes` only when every required criterion is `yes`.
+Summarize misunderstanding patterns without quoting identifying details.
+
+## Closure record
+
+| Cohort | Participants | Passes | Result | Date | Anonymized findings link |
+| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
+| Maintainers | 5 |  |  |  |  |
+| Contributors | 5 |  |  |  |  |
+| Operators/node runners | 5 |  |  |  |  |
+
+Do not claim comprehension validation is complete until anonymized results are attached.
+If any cohort has fewer than 4 of 5 passes, revise the promise or proof path and repeat that cohort with a new five-person sample.
